\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs22\lang9 Boris Johnson and the powers of the prime minister \f1\emdash  what can we\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 learn from recent events?\par
Boris Johnson went from winning a landslide election result in December 2019, to having 55\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 resignations in 48 hours in July 2022. He is one of the shortest serving prime ministers since 1900.\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 How did this happen? What does this show us about the fluctuating power of prime ministers?\par
\par
\f0\lang2057 **************************************************************************\f1\lang9\par
Johnson\rquote s leadership style\par
As PM, Johnson had an appeal that enabled him to appeal to voters who had not voted Conservative\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 before. Using charm and humour, rather than a close attention to detail, plus his commitment to Brexit,\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 he was able to win over working-class voters in the north, leading to the collapse of the Red Wall\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 seats in the 2019 election. Some have described him as a populist, a charismatic leader, promising to\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 break the rules and attack the establishment, for example in his attempted prorogation of Parliament.\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 This style of leadership may also have led to his downfall, for example in his vague and contradictory\f0\lang2057  r\f1\lang9 esponses to questions about Partygate.\par
\par
\f0\lang2057 **************************************************************************\f1\lang9\par
Johnson\rquote s relationship with his cabinet\par
Johnson\rquote s cabinet initially included his supporters and Brexiteers. There was little attempt to include a\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 broad section of the party, which has been the approach of previous PMs. However, it was the most\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 diverse cabinet in UK history, including Sajid Javid (his first chancellor), Rishi Sunak (his second\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 chancellor) and Priti Patel (home secretary). Johnson proved to be very supportive of ministers who\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 came under pressure from the media to resign, such as Priti Patel (bullying), Gavin Williamson (A\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 Level results algorithm) and Matt Hancock (PPE procurement). This led to a growing sense that the\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 convention of individual ministerial responsibility was under serious threat. His first task as PM was to\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 manage Brexit, and he set up a group of six ministers (all male) to do this. He cut the number of\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 cabinet committees, preferring a simpler structure to previous PMs (as advised by Dominic Cummings\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9\emdash  his chief aide and someone with little respect for traditions).\par
\par
\f0\lang2057 **************************************************************************\f1\lang9\par
Brexit and Parliament\par
Johnson initially took over a minority government, which he found extremely frustrating in terms of\f0\lang2057  Brexit \f1\lang9 negotiations. This led to his failed attempt to prorogue Parliament and clash with the Supreme\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 Court in September 2019. He then called for an election in December 2019 and managed to persuade\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 Parliament to back this, which required a two-thirds majority (under the Fixed Term Parliament Act,\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 which was later abolished). The UK left the EU on 31 January 2020 and entered a transition period.\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 This can be seen as the high point of Johnson\rquote s time as PM.\par
\par
\f0\lang2057 **************************************************************************\f1\lang9\par
The role of events: Covid\par
Johnson was initially criticised for his slow response. He then worked with three key ministers, again\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 all male \emdash  Sunak, Hancock and Gove, to deal with the crisis. Cabinet was sidelined during much of\f0\lang2057  \lang9 this period from March 2020 to Spring 2021. Johnson has been praised by some for his leadership,particularly relating to the vaccine rollout. However, he has also been criticised for the UK\rquote s high excess death rate, and later due to the Partygate revelations. The pandemic shows how the power of the PM can be significantly impacted by unexpected events.\par
\par
\lang2057 **************************************************************************\lang9\par
The role of events: Ukraine\par
The war in Ukraine initially proved very useful to Boris Johnson. He built a close personal relationship with President Zelensky and became seen in Ukraine as one of their strongest supporters on the world stage. This also helped Johnson at a time when the UK economy was struggling with the impact of Brexit, as he was able to show how leaving the EU allowed him to act swiftly and independently. He was also able to use the war as a justification for staying in office after the vote of confidence in June 2022: \lquote getting on with the job\rquote .\par
\par
\lang2057 **************************************************************************\lang9\par
Partygate and the return of sleaze\par
The PM and his chancellor were fined, along with 81 other people, for events that took place in Downing Street and other government buildings during the first lockdown. He initially refused to accept that these parties had taken place at all, claiming \lquote all guidance was followed\rquote . After the publication of the Sue Gray report, there was a confidence vote in his leadership in June 2022 which he won, although 41% of Conservative MPs voted against him. At this point, Johnson refused to resign. Revelations about the behaviour of deputy chief whip Chris Pincher damaged Johnson further, leading to mass resignations. Previous to this, there were several other very damaging events such as the behaviour of his chief aide Dominic Cummings, the \'a3112,000 refurbishment of his flat and the attempt to protect former minister Owen Paterson from resigning due to breaking lobbying rules. Sleaze and a sense of dishonesty were the key reasons why public opinion about Johnson changed so significantly.\par
\par
\lang2057 **************************************************************************\lang9\par
Byelections\par
The loss of Wakefield to Labour and Tiverton and Homerton to the Liberal Democrats in June 2022 led to the resignation of Oliver Dowden as chairman of the Conservative Party. Although governments often lose byelections, the swings away from the Conservatives were too large to ignore.\par
\par
\lang2057 **************************************************************************\lang9\par
The end\par
By the time Johnson resigned, 46 ministers, excluding junior members of the government, had quit over the course of his time as PM. This is even more than under Theresa May and shows that ultimately, even a PM with a large majority can be brought down by the cabinet. Ministers were no longer able to put up a united front under the principle of collective responsibility. Their loyalty evaporated when it appeared that Johnson was no longer a vote winner.\par
\par
\lang2057 **************************************************************************\lang9\par
How powerful was Boris Johnson?\par
Michael Foley\rquote s theory of \lquote spatial leadership\rquote  suggested that prime ministers are becoming more like directly elected presidents. Foley argued that prime ministers increasingly portrayed themselves as outsiders \f1\emdash  distancing themselves from Parliament and government. This can clearly be seen in\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 Johnson\rquote s resignation speech, where he described Conservative MPs as having a \lquote herd-like\rquote\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 mentality, and in his regular claims to have a direct mandate from the people.\f0\lang2057  \par
\f1\lang9 Secondly, Foley suggested that PMs were increasingly speaking directly to the media. Johnson is an\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 ex-journalist and was always very aware of the importance of his media image. For example, he\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 employed a personal photographer. Finally, Foley discussed the growing use of special advisers, such\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 as Dominic Cummings, who are unelected and report directly to the PM. However, it must be\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 remembered that Johnson was forced to resign by his cabinet, which suggests that the theory of\f0\lang2057  \lang9 cabinet government still has some relevance. Without their support, he was unable to continue, regardless of any supposed mandate from the people\par
